{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787151724,"nonce":"Pqt4mnSg8bW8gwvjdl8flgz62Zk=","title":"The truncation that exists to keep a Slack section under 3 000 characters can produce 3 013, because its \"see full detail in oto\" suffix embeds a URL it never measures — and the checked-in fixture is 4 bytes from tripping it","message":"**What is wrong.** `truncateSection` cuts a section's text at 2 900 bytes and appends `… \u003cURL|see full detail in oto\u003e`, relying on a fixed 100-byte allowance to keep the result inside Slack's 3 000-byte section limit. The suffix is not 100 bytes; it is **`len(URL) + 29`**. So the allowance holds only while the oto deep link is **71 bytes or shorter**, and nothing anywhere checks that. When it is longer, the function whose entire purpose is to enforce the limit returns a string that violates it, `Validate`'s V5 check rejects the payload, and `Render` returns a terminal error — so the delivery does not degrade, it **dies**.\n\n**The demonstration.** The constants, read directly: `internal/channels/render/slack/text.go:27` `maxSectionText = 3000`, `:101` `sectionTruncateAt = 2900` — whose own comment states the intent, *\"where a section is cut so that the ellipsis and the 'see full detail in oto' link still fit inside maxSectionText (§H.7)\"* — and `:141` `return truncateAt(text, sectionTruncateAt, maxSectionText, moreURL)`.\n\nThe suffix is built at `:153-156`: `suffix = \"… \" + link(moreURL, \"see full detail in oto\")`, and `link` at `:127-132` returns `\"\u003c\" + url + \"|\" + escape(label) + \"\u003e\"`. That is `\"… \"` (4 bytes — U+2026 is three) `+ 1 + len(url) + 1 + 22 + 1` = **`len(url) + 29`**. `truncateAt` returns `head + suffix` with `head` up to `cut`, and never measures the total against `hard`.\n\nWorked, with real values:\n\n```\nhttps://oto.example.com/groups/\u003cuuid\u003e                     67 bytes → 2900+96  = 2996  OK, 4 bytes spare\nhttp://localhost:8080/groups/\u003cuuid\u003e                       65 bytes → 2900+94  = 2994  OK\nhttps://oto.internal.example.company.com/groups/\u003cuuid\u003e    84 bytes → 2900+113 = 3013  V5 REJECTS\n```\n\nThe middle line is oto's shipped default `base_url`. The top line is what `test/harness/slack_cards.go` uses, so **the checked-in corpus sits four bytes from the cliff**. The bottom line is an ordinary corporate hostname, not a contrived one — `base_url` needs to be five characters longer than `https://oto.example.com` for a truncated section to start killing deliveries.\n\n`truncateField` has the same shape with a 120-byte allowance (`:146`, `maxFieldText-120`), so its threshold is 91 bytes.\n\n**Why it matters.** It fails in the worst direction. A too-long section could have been cut harder; instead the payload is refused whole, and §L.6 sends it to the dead letter with `slack render invalid (V5): block 0 section text is N chars, limit 3000`. The operator reading that message will look for a renderer bug in the card that was too long, not for a base URL that is nineteen characters longer than the one the constants were tuned against. It is also silent until it is total: every card under 3 000 bytes is unaffected, so this appears only for the long ones — a `rule_changed` reply carrying an expression diff, a long `comment`, a root card with many instances — which are exactly the cards an operator most wants to read.\n\n⭐ And the reason it has never been seen is that nothing in the corpus reaches it. Truncation only fires above 3 000 bytes, and every checked-in fixture is far below that; the two broadcast-eligible reply bodies are ~150 bytes. So the guard has never run in anger with a URL of any length.\n\n**Where.** `internal/channels/render/slack/text.go:140-142` (`truncateSection`), `:144-147` (`truncateField`), `:149-171` (`truncateAt`, which computes `suffix` and then never measures `head + suffix`), `:127-132` (`link`), `:27` and `:101` (the two constants and the comment asserting the invariant). The rejection lands at `internal/channels/render/slack/validate.go:216`. Callers that pass a URL: `reply.go`'s `truncateSection(body, v.Links.Group)` and `truncateField(extra, v.Links.Group)`, plus the root card's fields.\n\n**Scope.** small, and contained in one function. `truncateAt` should compute `cut` from `hard - len(suffix)` rather than take a caller's guess, which makes the invariant true by construction instead of by tuning — and then `sectionTruncateAt` and the `-120` both become derived rather than magic. A test asserting `len(truncateSection(x, url)) \u003c= maxSectionText` for a URL of 40, 71, 72 and 400 bytes is what proves it, and would have caught this. ⚠️ One decision: when the URL is so long that no useful head remains, the honest behaviours are \"drop the link and keep more text\" or \"keep the link and cut to almost nothing\" — that is a product call, and the second is arguably right because a truncated card without a way to the full detail is the defect `68653ca` was about.\n\n**Done when.** `truncateAt` measures its own suffix, `truncateSection` and `truncateField` cannot return more than their limit for any URL length, there is a test that pins it at the boundary, and the no-useful-head case has a recorded answer.\n\n⭐ Found by a red team attacking `17f8055`, which fed a ~4 000-byte `Links.Group` and got `slack render invalid (V5): block 0 section text is 4063 chars, limit 3000`. The 71-byte threshold above is mine, computed from the constants after reading them — the red team's probe suggested this needed an absurd URL, and the arithmetic says an ordinary one will do. Related: `3f5e952`, which is the missing validation on `base_url` that lets its length go unbounded in the first place.","files":null}]}